    <md:title>7.12.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d024e2f5-8c66-4e8a-b800-57f5a94a7d4e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="d21eb73e-0532-407c-8c17-041efa2bbf99"><problem><para>
      <para>Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/bd59d8abb03c9dac56adad4cf8fd4dc0d9bf2f64"/></media></figure></para>
    </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(f(x)=x^2+5\)</para><para>\(f(x)=x^2-3\)</para><para>\(f(x)=x^2\)</para><para>\(f(x)=x^2+7\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(f(x)=x^2-3\)</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=x^2-3\). The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2-3\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-3)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
